docs/vision-system/planning/reviews/F-integration-04.md:43:Checkout creation then carries a continuing exposure. Capacity remains held until native expiry or verified cancellation; an unknown creation does not release that hold. Actual product delivery requires observed public behavior, functional and accessibility evidence, export/recovery and beneficiary acceptance. Human service requires actual accepted performance. Statement reconciliation distinguishes processor activity, bank cash and professional closure. These are substantive fulfillment criteria, rather than labels around a generated packet. See [07](../specification/07-integrations-capacity.md), lines 48–51 and 99.
docs/vision-system/research/reviews/2026-09-12-substrate-controls.md:34:The strongest counterexample is a locally committed release row followed by interrupted replication: a second connection reads it and dispatches while the required independent evidence never existed.
docs/vision-system/research/reviews/2026-09-12-substrate-controls.md:38:**Source-level finding.** Both Linux v6.12 and current upstream `nft_meta.c` implement `NFT_META_TIME_NS` by calling `ktime_get_real_ns()` during expression evaluation. It reads current kernel realtime; it does not consult an immutable packet-arrival timestamp. Linux 6.12 remains a listed long-term kernel line, but an eventual distribution build and userspace version still require qualification. [Linux v6.12 implementation](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/net/netfilter/nft_meta.c), [current upstream implementation](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/net/netfilter/nft_meta.c), [kernel release status, dated February 25, 2026](https://www.kernel.org/category/releases.html).
docs/vision-system/research/reviews/2026-09-12-substrate-controls.md:44:**Encoding also needs verification.** The current manual describes integer Unix timestamps, while the nftables wiki—last edited March 28, 2024—describes integer nanoseconds. I inspected the released **nftables 1.1.3** source archive in memory: `src/meta.c`’s `date_type_parse` converts integer seconds to nanoseconds; date-string parsing applies a local timezone offset. Kernel nanosecond storage therefore does not imply that an arbitrary userspace timestamp literal has nanosecond resolution or UTC interpretation. [nftables manual](https://netfilter.org/projects/nftables/manpage.html), [time-selector wiki](https://wiki.nftables.org/wiki-nftables/index.php/Matching_packet_metainformation#Matching_by_time), [upstream 1.1.3 source archive](https://www.netfilter.org/projects/nftables/files/nftables-1.1.3.tar.xz).
docs/vision-system/research/F2/R1-context.md:77:**F-17. SOURCE CLAIM, vendor-conflicted, current generation, and the most surprising finding in this lane.** Anthropic's August 2026 multiagent research reports that in one experiment "18 out of 30 agents decided to create a git branch with the exact same branch name"; that in a job-queue experiment "there were 2.4 million job requests and only 117 jobs accepted"; that "transmitting context is about as costly as acting on it, and an agent can be forked or repurposed at will"; and that agents lack the social technologies humans use, having "reputation to lose, no court to appeal to, and no colleague who remembers them" (R1-S23). The same source reports that on a parallelizable task, "the simple independent parallelized method produces 21 vulnerabilities over a 6.5 million token run, while the coordinating agent swarm found 266 vulnerabilities over a 27 million token run", with "only 12 vulnerabilities in common between them", and reports that in hidden-profile tasks agents fail to surface private information contradicting group consensus.
