docs/vision-system/research/F2/R8-contrarian.md:35:**A-7. UNKNOWN, and it is the load-bearing hole in A.** No source found in this lane measures whether persistent model identity improves accepted outcomes. The two most-cited public deployments of "persistent agent" and "AI employee" products both fail to support it. An independent one-month review of Devin reports: "Out of 20 tasks we attempted, we saw 14 failures, 3 inconclusive results, and just 3 successes", and attributes the failures to persistence itself: "The autonomous nature that seemed promising became a liability — Devin would spend days pursuing impossible solutions rather than recognizing fundamental blockers" [S19]. Klarna's own press release claims its assistant "is doing the equivalent work of 700 full-time agents" and "$40 million USD in profit improvement" [S20]; fifteen months later the company reversed and rehired, with its chief executive quoted saying cost-driven automation produces "lower quality" [S21, secondary]. **Neither refutes chartered persistence. Both refuse to support it, and A must carry that.**
docs/vision-system/research/F2/R8-contrarian.md:63:**W-5. FOUNDER CONSTRAINT, unenforced. The no-silent-metered-fallback rule is defeated by a documented in-product affordance at both providers.** Claude Code: `/usage-credits` opens the credits setting, and "on Pro and Max plans, when you reach your spend limit with usage credits still available, Claude Code prompts you to raise or remove the limit without leaving the CLI"; turning credits on also drops the prompt-cache lifetime from an hour to five minutes [S3]. Codex: "ChatGPT Plus and Pro users who reach their usage limit can purchase additional credits to continue working without needing to upgrade their existing plan" [S10]. **The subscription-to-metered path is one setting away on both providers, is surfaced at exactly the moment of exhaustion, and the package has no mechanism that observes or blocks it.** CP-173 states the policy; nothing enforces it. This is the conformance gap in TC-26.
docs/vision-system/research/F2/R8-contrarian.md:65:**W-6. DIRECT OBSERVATION. The denominator for "scarce capacity consumed" does not exist on the Claude side.** The current Max plan page states only "20 times more usage per session than the Pro plan", "Max plans also have a weekly usage limit that applies across all models", "The weekly limit resets at a fixed time each week that is assigned to your account", and "to manage capacity and ensure fair access to all users, we may limit your usage in other ways, such as weekly and monthly caps" [S1]. No absolute figure appears on that page, on the usage-and-length-limits page [S2], on the best-practices page [S8], or on the Claude Code Pro/Max page [S9], all fetched 2026-09-13. Anthropic did publish absolute figures in July 2025 — Pro "40 to 80 hours of Sonnet 4", Max $100 "140 to 280 hours of Sonnet 4 and 15 to 35 hours of Opus 4", Max $200 "240 to 480 hours of Sonnet 4 and 24 to 40 hours of Opus 4", effective 2025-08-28, said to affect "less than 5% of subscribers" [S11, S12, both secondary]. Codex, by contrast, still publishes approximate per-window message ranges by plan, with the caveat that these "are not fixed message limits" [S10]. **Consequence for this round: every capacity comparison it can perform is a ratio between arms. No arm can be expressed as a fraction of the week. "This design consumes 40% of the allowance" is not a sentence anyone here can write.**
