docs/vision-system/research/F2/R5-routing.md:21:**R5-01. The founder's thesis describes a named 1970s architecture, and that architecture deliberately does not specify who decides what happens next. [Source claim + Inference]** The blackboard model is "a global database called the blackboard" with "logically independent sources of knowledge called the knowledge sources", and the figure caption states: "Note that there is no control flow; the knowledge sources are self-activating" [S27]. Its own author's footnote is the load-bearing sentence: "There is no control component specified in the blackboard model. The model merely specifies a general problem-solving behavior. The actual locus of control can be in the knowledge sources, on the blackboard, in a separate module, or in some combination of the three" [S27]. Nii also warns that "the blackboard model is a conceptual entity, not a computational specification" and that "a sketch is a long way from a working system" [S27]. **Inference:** "coordinated capabilities around a shared source of truth" is the blackboard model restated, and TC-41 is not an imprecision in the founder's wording. It is the exact hole the original model left open, unclosed for forty years.
docs/vision-system/research/F2/R5-routing.md:23:**R5-02. Adding a coordinator to a blackboard is not free, and the source says what it costs. [Source claim]** In Nii's jigsaw analogy a monitor is introduced to serialize access, and "the monitor has broad executive power. The monitor could, for example, force the puzzle to be solved systematically from left to right; that is, the monitor has the power to violate one essential characteristic of the original blackboard model, that of opportunistic problem solving" [S27]. **Inference:** the package's choice of an explicit admission authority (C02) is one of the three readings TC-41 names, and its cost is already documented: concentrated agenda authority can suppress the opportunism that made the shared record valuable. `02 §2` names that same cost independently.
docs/vision-system/research/F2/R5-routing.md:152:Verdict: **the package's answer is corroborated and the thesis's phrasing is under-specified.** Three properties appear in every working instance. It has a named author and a named approver, and those are different roles [S1]. It has an explicit validity window, the operational period, and is re-issued rather than edited [S1]. Only one component advances the cursor, which in Temporal is the engine and in the package is C02 [S12, `05 §3`]. Two further constraints come from the sources. Waiting for better information is prohibited: "Personnel should not delay planning meetings in anticipation of future information" [S1]. And the blackboard source warns that the view and the record must not be the same object, because a controller with the power to order the next action can override the opportunism the record exists to enable [S27]. **The thesis phrase "an updated view of what should happen next" names an artifact with no author, no approver and no expiry, and that is the (d)-class shape.**
