bligation.production_mode_named` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."},{"record":"Obligation","from":"potential","to":"recognized","findings":["F6C-01"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.obligation.production_mode_named` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."},{"record":"WorkflowRun","from":"acceptance_pending","to":"accepted","findings":["F6C-04","F6C-14"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.launch.actual_compared_to_expected` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."},{"record":"FailureRecord","from":"investigating","to":"blocked","findings":["F6C-09"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."},{"record":"FailureRecord","from":"observed","to":"blocked","findings":["F6C-09"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."},{"record":"FailureRecord","from":"retry_admitted","to":"blocked","findings":["F6C-09"],"why":"This edge is where `guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient` is attached. Deleting the TRANSITION is a different attack from deleting the call: the edge predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the route is gone (F6D-04)."}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/pinned_transitions"},{"id":"requires_waivers","why":"RC-03. `requires` and `conjuncts` are independent fields of one spec and nothing compared them, so a criterion could state one requirement and demand another -- which is what the weakened derivation produced: the sentence still promised delivery over a body that had stopped asking for it. Measured before any waiver: 774 criteria examined, 0 failing the field-path rule, 17 failing the phase-name rule, every one of the 17 a phase-kind synonym for the criterion's OWN phase. Each waiver below names the exact tokens it excuses, so a new unmatched phase on a waived criterion still fails. SCOPE, stated because the rule's name promises more than the rule does (RC2-03). What is compared is a FORMATTING-DEPENDENT SUBSET of the sentence: backticked lowercase tokens (PROSE_TOKEN) and field paths that are either backticked or written literally as /payload/... (PROSE_PATH). English nouns are not compared to conjuncts, and most of what these sentences are made of is English nouns. Removing three pairs of backticks leaves a sentence promising a reader exactly the same thing and invisible to the rule -- the recheck measured that, and the run went from stopping at RC-03 in 19 seconds to passing it and failing 640 seconds later for an unrelated reason. LIVE POPULATION, re-derived on the committed corpus with the disjunction-aware walker: 774 criteria examined; 0 of them (0.0%) name a field path in `requires`, so the PATHS rule -- the half RC-03's required contract actually named -- has an applicable population of ZERO and contributes no live assertion; 19 (2.5%) name a backticked phase of a related record, 22 tokens in all; 17 of those 19 rely on a waiver below, and all 17 waivers are in use; the remaining 5 tokens across 2 criteria (criterion.SalesAgreement.performed.v1: `delivered`, `discharged`, `transferred`; criterion.GrievanceCase.remedy_authorized.v1: `held`, `partly_consumed`) are bound by a conjunct and are the whole of the rule's live force. THE NARROW SCOPE IS THE ACCEPTED LIMIT, and the alternative was measured rather than argued. Widening the phase rule to unbackticked tokens of related records' phase vocabulary produces 425 findings across 266 of the 774 criteria (34%), over 62 distinct tokens -- case-sensitive, and already excluding every token a conjunct binds or a waiver excuses. The vocabulary is ordinary English past participles used as verbs: `recorded` 104 (\"the subject's own declared identity/scope fields are recorded\"), `required` 43 (\"no judgment is required\"), `current` 37, `admitted` 34, `assigned` 30 (\"a custodian is assigned\"). A waiver list of that size is not a waiver list, it is a second corpus, and a rule that demands one teaches contributors to route around the checker. So the rule keeps its scope, the number is written down here rather than left to be rediscovered, and what is added instead is the control the live half never had: fixtures/negative/r14-requires-names-a-related-phase-no-conjunct-binds. Until it, the only RC-03 fixture exercised the half with zero population. The cure for the formatting dependency is upstream and is not a checker change. A criterion's `requires` sentence and its conjuncts are written by one author in one entry of tools/phase_content.py, so what makes the rule reach is the author backticking the phases the sentence promises. That is a CONVENTION, nothing enforces it, and this sentence exists so that nobody reads the rule's name as though something did.","value":[{"criterion":"criterion.AgentTemplate.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while AgentTemplate spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.BuildArtifact.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while BuildArtifact spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.CasePool.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while CasePool spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.ConfigurationVersion.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while ConfigurationVersion spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.ContentAsset.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while ContentAsset spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.DecisionPacket.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while DecisionPacket spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.Defect.verified_repaired.v1","phases":["repaired"],"why":"The sentence names `repaired`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while Defect spells the same phase `verified_repaired`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.Deployment.effect_unknown.v1","phases":["unknown_effect"],"why":"The sentence names `unknown_effect`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while Deployment spells the same phase `effect_unknown`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.EvaluationPlan.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while EvaluationPlan spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.EvaluatorProfile.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while EvaluatorProfile spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.ForgetRequest.verified_with_limits.v1","phases":["verified"],"why":"The sentence names `verified`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while ForgetRequest spells the same phase `verified_with_limits`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.InstructionBundle.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while InstructionBundle spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.JournalBatch.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while JournalBatch spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.Offer.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while Offer spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.RetrievalProfile.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while RetrievalProfile spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.SkillVersion.draft.v1","phases":["proposed"],"why":"The sentence names `proposed`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while SkillVersion spells the same phase `draft`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."},{"criterion":"criterion.WorkOrder.report_submitted.v1","phases":["reported"],"why":"The sentence names `reported`, which is the PHASE-KIND entry this content was authored from, while WorkOrder spells the same phase `report_submitted`. So the token is a second name for the criterion's own phase and not a requirement about a related record -- there is nothing for a `related_phases` binding to hold. A reviewer can dispute this, and the dispute has a cure: make the kind-level prose in tools/phase_content.py say the record's own phase name. That is 17 sentences across the corpus and it was out of scope here; what is NOT in dispute is that the body demands what the sentence demands."}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/requires_waivers"},{"id":"pinned_attachments","why":"F6D-01, and it is the SECOND half of a pin. A conjunct pin holds a guard to what its BODY must demand and says nothing about whether any edge CALLS it -- the file said so itself, one section down, about transitions. The measurement: one detachment fixture per guard, every call site removed, thirty runs, THIRTY UNDETECTED AND ZERO CAUGHT. Deleting `{\"op\":\"call\",\"predicate_id\":\"guard....\"}` from an edge body is a one-line edit, the guard definition survives byte for byte, every conjunct pin still passes over it, and the whole apparatus reports green. Detaching two guards restores AT-M4-02; detaching one restores AS-X-01; detaching two restores the skill-grant path of R2 F-08.\nThis table is HAND-WRITTEN and is derived from nothing, for the same reason `pinned_transitions` is: a table computed from the registry it checks agrees with every edit to that registry. Each row names a guard and the exact edges that must call it, and the `why` says what detaching it restores -- a row whose reason is 'it should be attached' would be a row nobody can evaluate.\nThe check demands the call be DEMANDED, not merely present: a call inside a value slot, under a `not`, under an unopted disjunction or under a quantifier is a call the edge's truth does not depend on, which is the RC3-01/RC3-02 class one layer out. And every `guard.*` predicate in the registry must appear here exactly once, so a guard registered tomorrow with no attachment fails rather than arriving silently unattached.","value":[{"guard":"guard.acceptance.resolution_reason_present","edges":["edge.AcceptanceInterval.in_acceptance.accepted.v1","edge.AcceptanceInterval.in_acceptance.rejected.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"An acceptance or a rejection with an empty resolution reason is the silent close section 9 row 14 refuses. Detached, both terminal edges of AcceptanceInterval accept an empty reason and the record still reads `accepted`."},{"guard":"guard.admission.acceptance_role_staffed","edges":["edge.AdmissionRecord.opened.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, work is admitted into a duty class whose acceptance role is unfilled -- R-X09's whole case, and section 9 row 12. The refusal has to happen at admission, because the moment the acceptance falls due is too late to staff it."},{"guard":"guard.admission.refusal_names_failed_predicate","edges":["edge.AdmissionRecord.opened.refused.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a refusal need name no failed predicate, and silence becomes refusal. This guard also carries the false-exclusion half -- unfamiliar wording is not a predicate -- so removing it loses the control in both directions at once."},{"guard":"guard.approval.expiry_present","edges":["edge.ExistenceJustification.proposed.admitted.v1","edge.ProjectionGrant.issued.active.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Two edges, and both are needed: an approval with no expiry is a standing grant nobody renewed, on ExistenceJustification and on ProjectionGrant alike."},{"guard":"guard.branch.no_model_selected_acceptance_strength","edges":["edge.StepExecution.dispatched.running.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a model-authored typed value selects between acceptance strengths -- R-X19 and AS-M3-01 -- and the producing side picks how hard its own work will be checked."},{"guard":"guard.calibration.current_for_checker","edges":["edge.AcceptanceInterval.in_acceptance.accepted.v1"],"findings":["F6B-03"],"why":"F6B-03. Detached, an acceptance commits on a checker with no calibration in `run` at all -- which is the state EVERY checker is in before its first calibration session, so the rule would be unenforced exactly when it matters most. No record in the registry related to `InstrumentCalibration`: the clause naming the attachment point was the one clause with no attachment."},{"guard":"guard.check.reads_only_non_authorable_fields","edges":["edge.StandingInterest.trial.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a check interest's precondition reads fields the producer authored, so the producer decides whether it is checked."},{"guard":"guard.check.tie_break_strongest_sufficient","edges":["edge.StandingInterest.trial.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the tie-break reverts to weakest-sufficient, which is the direction R-X20 exists to invert."},{"guard":"guard.class.no_total_rank","edges":["edge.ConsequenceDerivation.computed.recomputed_at_release.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the derivation may declare an ordering and a rank returns. AT-M4-02 measured what that costs: one class computed from the set, one row applied, and C2's human requirement dropped on an action that is both C2 and C3. It is also the guard whose `unique` on reached_class_ids is what keeps the sibling gate check honest on the same edge."},{"guard":"guard.class.reached_set_gates_all","edges":["edge.ConsequenceDerivation.computed.recomputed_at_release.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the gate set may cover a proper subset of the reached set and the derivation still advances. This is the positive half of the same finding: no rank, AND every reached class gated."},{"guard":"guard.constraint.set_bound_at_transfer","edges":["edge.Continuation.prepared.offered.v1","edge.DecisionPacket.ready.presented.v1","edge.OperatorProjection.incomplete.current.v1","edge.OperatorProjection.stale.current.v1","edge.OperatorProjection.unknown.current.v1"],"findings":["F6B-02"],"why":"F6B-02. Five edges over three carriers. `constraint_set_ref` occurred TWICE in the whole schema and on exactly one record, `Delegation`, where it was not required -- so R-X27's rule that a constraint set is required on every transfer the fixtures name, INCLUDING the continuation and the founder-facing brief, bound one boundary out of six and optionally there. Detached from the OperatorProjection edges in particular, the founder is back on the untyped side of the boundary, which is uniform gap G-05 and AX-M1-01."},{"guard":"guard.constraint.standing_interest_writes_proposed_only","edges":["edge.StandingInterest.trial.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a standing interest writes outside `proposed` -- it stops being a proposer and becomes an actor, with no admission in between."},{"guard":"guard.criteria.author_not_in_producing_path","edges":["edge.AcceptanceInterval.declared_done.in_acceptance.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"AS-X-01, the round's only (d) landing on W5. Detached, the producing path authors the criteria its own work is accepted against. The review called the criteria pair the clearest close in the whole amendment; both halves live on one edge and both are one deletion away."},{"guard":"guard.criteria.frozen_before_producing_step","edges":["edge.AcceptanceInterval.declared_done.in_acceptance.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"The other half: detached, criteria are amended between admission and submission, which is the same defeat arriving later."},{"guard":"guard.custody.admitter_excluded","edges":["edge.AdmissionRecord.opened.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the ordered custody resolver may return the admitting identity, so the party that admitted the work also holds custody of it (R-X06)."},{"guard":"guard.delegation.width_ceiling_over_distinct_read_sets","edges":["edge.StepExecution.dispatched.running.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, width is bounded by cost again and fifty sequential consultations each individually cheap sit inside one budget -- AS-X-04, and section 9 row 8."},{"guard":"guard.derivation.recompute_matches","edges":["edge.ConsequenceDerivation.recomputed_at_release.matched.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, `recomputed_at_release -> matched` asserts only that a recomputation happened. The release then proceeds on a set nobody compared to the admitted one, which is AT-M4-02 through the recompute path."},{"guard":"guard.derivation.writer_is_release_path","edges":["edge.ConsequenceDerivation.computed.recomputed_at_release.v1","edge.ConsequenceDerivation.recomputed_at_release.parked_on_mismatch.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Two edges, and the second is the park route. Detached, the derivation is writable outside the release path and its append-only rule has nobody enforcing it."},{"guard":"guard.effect.identity_allocated_before_release","edges":["edge.EffectIdentity.allocated.claimed.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a claim may precede its own allocation and the allocating component need not be C04 -- so the identity that is supposed to make a duplicate external effect detectable is written by whoever gets there first."},{"guard":"guard.effect.one_per_step","edges":["edge.StepExecution.running.reported.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a step emits more than one effect and the step stops being the idempotency unit. Every retry story in section 6 rests on that being true."},{"guard":"guard.effect.second_claimant_inherits","edges":["edge.EffectIdentity.allocated.claimed.v1"],"findings":["F6D-11"],"why":"F6D-11. Detached, `allocated -> claimed` asserts only that a claim happened, and the answer 05 section 6 gives to the protocol's tenth (d)-class example -- no stated winner between concurrent attempts on one work order -- is carried by nothing. The sibling guard is sound and is credited: it constrains ONE record's internal consistency, `allocated_at < claimed_at` and an allocating component of S1-C04, both of which are true of each racer independently."},{"guard":"guard.failure.no_progress_names_recipient","edges":["edge.FailureRecord.investigating.blocked.v1","edge.FailureRecord.observed.blocked.v1","edge.FailureRecord.retry_admitted.blocked.v1"],"findings":["F6C-09"],"why":"F6C-09. All three routes into `blocked`, which is the terminal phase a no-progress failure reaches. Detached, ALL ELEVEN FailureRecord transitions call no guard again -- which was the measured state -- and a record with `error_class: no_progress` and no recipient passes schema validation and every registered edge. That is the silent drop the rule names, reachable through the rule's own record."},{"guard":"guard.grant.declared_resolves_to_live_grant","edges":["edge.StepExecution.dispatched.running.v1","edge.WorkflowDefinition.draft.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-06"],"why":"F6D-06. Two admission edges, because both records declare the field. Detached, `declared_grants` is read by nothing again -- the string occurred in 0 predicate bodies when the review measured it -- and \"a declared grant is resolved against the grant policy at admission\" is a sentence about a resolution nobody performs. That is the decorative-declaration loophole the rule was written to close, reproduced by the rule: 44 capability declarations that nothing backed is the observed instance (R3 F57, F60)."},{"guard":"guard.holder.no_veto","edges":["edge.StandingHolder.staffed.suspended.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a holder's stated minimum becomes consent rather than evidence, and a protected lane holder can refuse a shed outright -- AE-M4-01, section 9 row 9."},{"guard":"guard.holder.not_retired_while_duty_live","edges":["edge.StandingHolder.staffed.retired_into.v1","edge.StandingHolder.suspended.retired_into.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Two edges, `staffed` and `suspended`, because retirement is reachable from both. Detached, a holder retires while a duty it carries is live and the address the duty was routed to stops existing."},{"guard":"guard.intake.per_origin_subcap_preserves_intake","edges":["edge.AdmissionRecord.opened.parked.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, cap exhaustion refuses the next legitimate complainant instead of overflowing to a named destination with an alarm and a reader. R-X17's point is that the failure mode of an intake cap is silence toward the person with the grievance."},{"guard":"guard.interest.precondition_invokes_no_model","edges":["edge.StandingInterest.proposed.trial.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"THE HARD RULE OF LAYER 3, and the layer's stated withdrawal condition is that it is violated. Detached, a standing interest's precondition invokes a model and the layer that exists to be deterministic is not."},{"guard":"guard.interest.writer_keys_not_self_declared","edges":["edge.StandingInterest.proposed.trial.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, an interest declares its own writer key, which is authority granting itself authority -- AS-M5-02."},{"guard":"guard.launch.actual_compared_to_expected","edges":["edge.WorkflowRun.acceptance_pending.accepted.v1"],"findings":["F6C-04","F6C-14"],"why":"F6C-04, F6C-14. The run's only route into `accepted`, which is what `* -> accepted` means for this record. Detached, no criterion compares actual launches to expected at run close, and reopen condition 4 of 02 section 9 -- changed provider terms or semantics -- names an instrument that does not exist, so the condition cannot fire. Under W9's own probe, swap the model provider, the package's stated detector would be absent and a changed provider property is noticed only by whatever else happens to break."},{"guard":"guard.launcher.stripping_positive_control","edges":["edge.SkillVersion.trial.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the probe skill's arrival at the enforcement point stops being a failure, and grant REMOVAL loses the positive control that grant delivery has. With the sibling guard also detached the whole skill-grant path of R2 F-08 and R6 F7 is restored."},{"guard":"guard.manifest.omission_and_class_declared","edges":["edge.ContextManifest.captured.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6B-01"],"why":"F6B-01. Detached, two of the three fields 06 section 2 requires of a manifest are required by nothing again: `dropped_constraints` was named by 0 predicates and `assembled_for_class` by 0 in the shipped registry -- its only appearance outside the schema was inside a benign fixture that inserts a presence check and throws it away when the run ends. A field whose only requirement lives in a discarded mutation is not required."},{"guard":"guard.obligation.production_mode_named","edges":["edge.Obligation.not_arisen.recognized.v1","edge.Obligation.potential.recognized.v1"],"findings":["F6C-01"],"why":"F6C-01. Both routes into `recognized`, because an obligation that was `not_arisen` and then arises is admitted by the second one and would otherwise be the way round the first. Detached, no predicate can refuse the admission of a due obligation in an exhaustible class that has no performer -- which was the measured state, uniform gap G-01 answered in prose only."},{"guard":"guard.protected.control_object_change_authorized","edges":["edge.ConfigurationVersion.draft.admitted.v1","edge.ConsequenceClassDefinition.admitted.restricted.v1","edge.ConsequenceClassDefinition.admitted.retired.v1","edge.ConsequenceClassDefinition.proposed.admitted.v1","edge.FieldAuthority.current.superseded.v1","edge.FieldAuthority.declared.current.v1","edge.StandingHolder.proposed.staffed.v1","edge.StandingHolder.staffed.retired_into.v1","edge.StandingHolder.staffed.suspended.v1"],"findings":["F6D-07"],"why":"F6D-07. Nine edges, one per row of 08 section 1's table, and the count is the finding: before this, NO edge on any of the six control objects referenced `ProtectedChange` at all, and three of the six were not records. Detached from any one of them, the object that decides who may act, who is reachable for a duty or who may write a record changes through an ordinary transition owned by an ordinary component -- which is the W10 self-modification probe resolving the wrong way."},{"guard":"guard.readset.delivered_equals_declared","edges":["edge.ContextManifest.captured.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, the loader's record no longer has to agree with the step's declaration, and negative control 6 -- an independent reviewer fed the producer's rationale -- is undetected at the one place the design says it is detected."},{"guard":"guard.reservation.lapse_independent_of_consent","edges":["edge.Reservation.held.released.v1","edge.Reservation.partly_consumed.released.v1","edge.Reservation.uncertain.released.v1"],"findings":["F6C-03","F6D-01"],"why":"F6C-03. THREE release edges, because a reservation reaches `released` from `held`, from `partly_consumed` and from `uncertain`, and the guard was on the first alone -- so a partly-consumed lane could be released without recording the lapsed unconsumed quantity, which is the quantity R-X24 says makes a never-consumed lane visible. Detached from any one of them, that route reopens. Section 9 row 10 is the case: a reservation unconsumed at the lapse point, held past it."},{"guard":"guard.skill.declared_grant_inert","edges":["edge.SkillVersion.trial.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a skill's declared tool grant stops being inert and the frontmatter grants tools for the turn that loads it -- the named runtime behaviour this rule was made structural to survive."},{"guard":"guard.supersession.walks_derived_closure","edges":["edge.FieldAuthority.current.superseded.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a supersession advances without walking the closure at all, so an accepted outcome standing on a superseded source is never reopened and nobody learns that it was not."},{"guard":"guard.trust.derived_is_floor_of_inputs","edges":["edge.ContextManifest.captured.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, a derived item's trust level may exceed its inputs', which is laundering by derivation."},{"guard":"guard.trust.promotion_is_an_effect_with_a_non_model_discriminator","edges":["edge.ContextManifest.captured.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6D-01"],"why":"Detached, an activation re-reading its own output promotes it -- section 9 row 5, and the guard is the only thing that makes promotion an effect rather than a re-read."},{"guard":"guard.workorder.critical_fields_authority_excludes_the_resuming_attempt","edges":["edge.WorkOrder.proposed.admitted.v1"],"findings":["F6C-13"],"why":"Detached, a work order is admitted with no marking authority fixed at admission, and R-D18's rule -- the capability owner at procedure admission, never the resuming attempt -- holds in prose only, which is the state F6C-13 measured: `critical_fields` in ZERO predicates. The refusal has to happen at admission, because after the attempt resumes the party with an interest in the answer is the one still able to write the marking."}],"source":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json#/pinned_attachments"},{"id":"answered_elsewhere","why":null,"value":{"F6A-10":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"REGISTRY AND SCHEMA DISAGREE ABOUT A CLOSED ENUM'S MEMBERS (57 pairs, with a floor under the walk) and THE ORDERED CUSTODY RESOLVER CANNOT RECORD A REASON THE CHAPTERS INSTRUCT (the hand-written CUSTODY_TIE_BREAK_REASONS literal). Fixtures: negative/r20-custody-tie-break-enum-omits-the-admitter-exclusion, positive/r20-custody-tie-break-enum-reordered-benign."},"F6D-09":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"A FIELD THAT NAMES A PREDICATE IS A FREE STRING (the PREDICATE_BEARING_FIELDS literal) and THE EVALUATOR KIND A GUARD COMPARES IS AN OPEN STRING. The review's third clause -- a conjunct on each named predicate's `implementation_status` -- is OWED, not delivered: that field holds one value across all 2,397 predicates, so the conjunct would be vacuous. The check THE PREDICATE REGISTRY NOW CLASSIFIES IMPLEMENTATION STATUS fails the moment that stops being true. Fixtures: negative/r22-refusal-names-a-predicate-that-is-only-a-string, positive/r22-precondition-ids-reordered-benign."},"F6R-03":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"A STEP 6 FINDING IS RAISED IN A REVIEW AND HELD BY NO SOURCE ROW, plus the floor under its own sweep (STEP 6 SWEEP OF THE F2-06 REVIEWS RETURNED ALMOST NOTHING)."},"F6D-08":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"A COMPUTED PROJECTION HAS A PERMITTED COMMAND (mutation m4's refusal, over the hand-written computed_projections table), A COMPUTED PROJECTION GAINED A LIFECYCLE TRANSITION, A PROJECTION'S EVALUATOR IS NOT A REGISTERED MODULE (the evaluator binding), A DECLARED PROJECTION WRITER SET CHANGED IN ONE PLACE and A PROJECTION RECORD IS IN NEITHER HALF OF THE PARTITION (completeness over lifecycle.projection). The custodial reading of owner_component is stated in record-registry.json at ArmedSet.owner_assignment and in pinned-conjuncts.json#/computed_projections_why; the 05 section 7 sentence is OWED and recorded in F2/07-repair-names-contract.md. Fixtures: negative/r34-armed-set-gains-the-three-kernel-record-commands, positive/r34-computed-projection-table-reordered-benign."},"F6AA-08":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"The three Step 6 vacuity floors, re-measured and set tight at the committed corpus: 101 ids mentioned, 100 raised, 11 families (they read 81 and 8, with no floor on the raised set at all). Fixtures: negative/r38-a-two-letter-family-finding-loses-its-source-row and positive/r38-a-two-letter-family-finding-is-declared-benign."},"F6AA-09":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/pinned-conjuncts.json","check":"F6Y-01 moved out of `not_answered` into this table, where its own row said its repairs belong, with the file and the two checks named. Enforced by \"A FINDING IS DECLARED ANSWERED WITH NO FILE AND NO CHECK NAMED\" and the file-existence check beside it in validate_contracts.py, and by the partition equality above them."},"F6Y-01":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"The STEP6_FAMILIES containment assertion (\"A STEP 6 REVIEW RAISES A FINDING IN AN UNDECLARED FAMILY\") and the widened per-id sweep (\"A STEP 6 FINDING IS RAISED IN A REVIEW AND HELD BY NO SOURCE ROW\"), with the fixture pair negative/r36-step-6-finding-loses-its-source-row and positive/r36-a-declared-family-gains-a-finding-benign. Moved here from `not_answered`, where its own row stated that this lane's repairs \"are named in `answered_elsewhere` individually where a file and a check exist\" -- a condition the row itself met (F6AA-09)."},"F6A-09":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"A CANDIDATE-ERA NAME IS A REGISTERED VALUE TYPE, with the fixture pair negative/r30-alias-key-is-a-registered-value-type and positive/r30-alias-table-gains-a-candidate-only-name-benign. Moved here from `not_answered`, whose boilerplate said no file-and-check pair was recorded -- true of the record and not of the tree (F6Y-08)."},"F6C-06":{"file":"planning/specification/contracts/validate_contracts.py","check":"A REGISTERED CAPACITY MEASURE IS NAMED BY NO CHAPTE