docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/MANIFEST.json:80:    "r18-second-claimant-re-releases",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event.json:2:  "id": "r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event.json:5:  "case": "A reservation is released with no event behind its lapse, so passing the lapse point is a comparison nobody made.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event.json:6:  "paired_benign_fixture": "fixtures/positive/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event-benign.json",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-guard-detached-from-its-edge.json:7:  "why": "The review swept this: one detachment fixture per guard, every call site removed, thirty runs, THIRTY UNDETECTED AND ZERO CAUGHT. This is the sharpest instance. `guard.class.no_total_rank` detached from `edge.ConsequenceDerivation.computed.recomputed_at_release.v1` restores AT-M4-02 -- the derivation may declare an ordering, a rank returns, and C2's human requirement is dropped on an action that is both C2 and C3 -- and it also removes the `unique` on reached_class_ids that keeps the sibling gate check on the same edge honest. A conjunct pin holds a guard to what its BODY demands and says nothing about whether any edge calls it; pinned_attachments is the half that does.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-guard-detached-from-its-edge.json:12:      "pointer": "/edge.ConsequenceDerivation.computed.recomputed_at_release.v1/body/predicates/3",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-load-bearing-edge-deleted.json:7:  "why": "Measured at bcfd658: three edge deletions on the new records, three runs, three exit 0. This is the consequential one. With `recomputed_at_release -> parked_on_mismatch` gone, that phase has exactly one outbound edge, to `matched`, so a recompute that disagrees with the admitted class set has nowhere to go but release. `parked_on_mismatch` is the phase whose row reads \"it never resolves downward\"; unreachable, it never resolves at all. Deleting the transition is a different attack from deleting the call: the predicate survives, the attachment pin still resolves, and only the ROUTE is gone.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-derivation-written-outside-the-release-path-benign.json:2:  "id": "r18-derivation-written-outside-the-release-path-benign",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-derivation-written-outside-the-release-path-benign.json:5:  "case": "A derivation written by the release path and marked append-only, which must not be refused.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-derivation-written-outside-the-release-path-benign.json:6:  "paired_adverse_fixture": "fixtures/negative/r18-derivation-written-outside-the-release-path.json",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-second-claimant-re-releases.json:2:  "id": "r18-second-claimant-re-releases",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-second-claimant-re-releases.json:5:  "case": "A second claimant re-releases instead of inheriting the first claimant's outcome.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-second-claimant-re-releases.json:6:  "paired_benign_fixture": "fixtures/positive/r18-second-claimant-re-releases-benign.json",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r16-10-reservation-held-past-its-lapse-point-benign.json:5:  "case": "A reservation consumed within the window, which must not be released early.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r16-10-reservation-held-past-its-lapse-point-benign.json:7:  "why": "A lane consumed inside its window still names its holder. Demanding the holder reference must not release it early -- the lapse rule binds at the lapse point and nowhere before it.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/negative/r18-effect-identity-triple-unconstrained.json:5:  "case": "The business identity triple carries no uniqueness constraint, so two concurrent allocations both validate and both release.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-guard-detached-from-its-edge-benign.json:7:  "why": "The attachment table states where a guard MUST be called, not where it may be. A check that also refused an extra call site would refuse the ordinary way a boundary is widened -- and three rows of the table already name two edges and one names three, so the table would be contradicting itself. Here `guard.derivation.writer_is_release_path` is additionally demanded on `recomputed_at_release -> matched`, which is a strengthening: the release path is the only writer on that edge too.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-guard-detached-from-its-edge-benign.json:12:      "pointer": "/edge.ConsequenceDerivation.recomputed_at_release.matched.v1/body/predicates/3",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-guard-detached-from-its-edge-benign.json:16:        "predicate_id": "guard.derivation.writer_is_release_path",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event-benign.json:2:  "id": "r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event-benign",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event-benign.json:6:  "paired_adverse_fixture": "fixtures/negative/r18-reservation-released-on-a-route-with-no-lapse-event.json",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r42-the-natural-key-create-path-rows-reordered-benign.json:39:            "counterparty_id",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r42-the-natural-key-create-path-rows-reordered-benign.json:44:          "why": "05 section 6 answers the tenth (d)-class example of the protocol by having a second claimant JOIN the first and inherit its outcome. That answer is unreachable unless the create path resolves: two rows for one triple is a race and a second release."
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-effect-identity-claimed-before-it-was-allocated-benign.json:7:  "why": "Every ordinary release allocates and then claims. The patch also demands the reserved name range be named, which C04 allocates centrally, and it must pass.",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-effect-identity-claimed-before-it-was-allocated-benign.json:12:      "pointer": "/guard.effect.identity_allocated_before_release/body/predicates/3",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-second-claimant-re-releases-benign.json:2:  "id": "r18-second-claimant-re-releases-benign",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-second-claimant-re-releases-benign.json:6:  "paired_adverse_fixture": "fixtures/negative/r18-second-claimant-re-releases.json",
docs/vision-system/planning/specification/contracts/fixtures/positive/r18-second-claimant-re-releases-benign.json:7:  "why": "The chapter names both columns: the adverse case is a second claimant that re-releases and the benign case is a first claimant releasing once. At the registry layer the benign case is a guard that may be hardened without the pin objecting -- here the allocation timestamp must also be present on the record being claimed, which every legitimate first claim carries. A control that refused this would refuse the ordinary release path, and the adverse count beside it would not be a detection rate.",
